    mechanism: 'The primary faces require flatness, compatible materials and stable lubrication. Secondary seals, gland plate, springs, drive elements and sleeve complete the assembly. Face opening or flashing can cause heat and wear; too much closing force can also overheat faces. Single, double, tandem and cartridge arrangements provide different containment and support options.',
    equations: ['seal chamber pressure, vapour pressure and temperature influence flash risk at the faces', 'seal balance changes the hydraulic closing force on the faces', 'support-fluid pressure, temperature and circulation are specified for dual-seal arrangements', 'shaft runout, axial movement and sleeve condition must remain within seal limits'],
    evidence: ['process fluid composition, solids, viscosity, vapour pressure and crystallisation tendency', 'pump pressure, temperature, speed, shaft size and seal-chamber geometry', 'available flush, quench, barrier or buffer fluid and utility reliability', 'seal materials, elastomer compatibility and corrosion conditions', 'leakage history, failure evidence and maintenance practices'],
    design: ['select face and elastomer materials for the real fluid, including cleaning and upset chemicals', 'provide the correct seal support plan where heat, solids, toxicity or vapour pressure demands it', 'install a sleeve and seal chamber with acceptable runout and surface finish', 'align the pump before coupling finalisation and remove pipe strain', 'trend support pressure, temperature, level and leakage rather than waiting for visible failure'],
    warning: ['dry running can destroy seal faces rapidly', 'a flush that is incompatible or too cold can crystallise product or create thermal shock', 'poor coupling alignment increases shaft movement at the faces', 'substituting seal parts without material review can create an immediate chemical-compatibility failure'],
    use: 'centrifugal pumps, mixers, agitators, reactors and rotating process equipment handling liquids',
    case: 'A hot hydrocarbon pump seal that leaks after a process change may not need a stronger spring. The review should confirm vapour margin in the seal chamber, flush path, face material, quench condition, shaft movement and whether the new fluid carries solids or polymerising components.',
